WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.028
Metadata checks can help a program confirm
facts such as file type, owner,

2
00:00:04.108 --> 00:00:07.579
group, permissions, size, or timestamps
before a sensitive operation.

3
00:00:07.659 --> 00:00:11.300
These checks are useful when the workflow
depends on properties of the file,

4
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.600
but they should be paired with careful
design.

5
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.638
A metadata check is not automatically a
permanent guarantee.

6
00:00:16.718 --> 00:00:17.913
File systems can change.

7
00:00:17.993 --> 00:00:20.554
A program may check a path and then later
open, modify,

8
00:00:20.634 --> 00:00:22.227
or delete something at that path.

9
00:00:22.307 --> 00:00:25.323
If another process can change the file or
directory in between,

10
00:00:25.403 --> 00:00:26.882
the earlier check may be stale.

11
00:00:26.962 --> 00:00:30.888
Sensitive workflows should reduce the gap
between check and use and prefer stable

12
00:00:30.968 --> 00:00:33.074
references where the platform offers them.

13
00:00:33.154 --> 00:00:35.600
Changing permissions should also be
intentional.

14
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.013
The program should know why the change is
needed,

15
00:00:38.093 --> 00:00:41.906
what permission state is expected
afterward, and what to do if the change fails.

16
00:00:41.986 --> 00:00:46.139
Silent permission-change failures are
dangerous because later code may proceed as if

17
00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:47.926
the file is protected when it is not.

18
00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:49.543
Where the platform supports it,

19
00:00:49.623 --> 00:00:53.549
changing permissions through a stable file
reference can be safer than reasoning

20
00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:54.824
only about a path string.

21
00:00:54.904 --> 00:00:58.659
The exact mechanism is platform-specific,
but the design goal is consistent:

22
00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:00.389
act on the object you mean to act on,

23
00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:03.713
and avoid assumptions that a path still
names the same object later.

24
00:01:03.793 --> 00:01:05.898
Permission management should fail safely.

25
00:01:05.978 --> 00:01:09.392
If metadata does not match expectations,
if a permission change fails,

26
00:01:09.472 --> 00:01:12.602
or if the program cannot confirm a
security-sensitive property,

27
00:01:12.682 --> 00:01:16.380
it should stop the sensitive workflow or
fall back to a clearly safe behavior.

28
00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.045
The code should not continue with a vague
warning while treating the file as

29
00:01:20.125 --> 00:01:20.580
trusted.
